.TH UNISCRIPT 1 "October 2026" "uniscript 1.0.5" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
uniscript \- ASCII names for Unicode text and back
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uniscript
[\fB\-r\fR] [\fB\-\-strict\fR|\fB\-\-lenient\fR] [\fB\-\-html\fR] [\fB\-\-explicit\fR] [\fItext\fR|\fI/path/file\fR]
.br
.B uniscript names
.br
.B uniscript build
[\fIentities/\fR] [\fIentities.idx\fR]
.br
.B uniscript check
[\fIentities/\fR] [\fIentities.idx\fR]
.br
.B uniscript chunks
[\fIentities.idx\fR] [\fIchunks/\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uniscript
converts names like \fB\e:alpha\fR or \fB<:fracture A/>\fR into the Unicode text they name
(\fBα 𝔄\fR). Without a text argument it reads standard input. Arguments starting with
\fB/\fR are files whose content is converted. A bare inline tag like \fB<:alpha>\fR converts too, with a
warning: it looks like an opening tag (\fB<:greek> athos <:/greek>\fR).
.PP
\fB./.uniscript\fR, those of its parent directories and \fB~/.uniscript\fR add or override names
(\fBvirus: 🦠\fR, the format of the entity files).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-r
the other direction: Unicode text back into names.
.TP
.B \-\-strict
unsupported characters are errors.
.TP
.B \-\-lenient
no errors: unknown names stay as written, with a warning.
.TP
.B \-\-html
meta information as <span lang style> instead of tag sequences.
.TP
.B \-\-explicit
rewrite inline tags, which warn, in their explicit form: \fB<:alpha>\fR becomes \fB\e:alpha\fR.
.TP
.B names
every name with its text, tab separated.
.TP
.B build
rebuild the name index from the readable entity files.
.TP
.B check
verify that the entity files and the index agree.
.TP
.B chunks
cut the index into chunks a web page loads on demand.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
uniscript "\e:alpha <:fracture A/>"      # α 𝔄
uniscript \-r "α 𝔄"                      # \e:alpha \e:fracture-A
uniscript \-\-explicit "<:alpha>"         # \e:alpha
echo "<:beside 犭 句/>" | uniscript      # ⿰犭句
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
https://pannous.com/uniscript/
